Iron is important in making red blood cells, which carry oxygen around the body (http://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron). Good food sources include liver (avoid during pregnancy), red meat, beans such as red kidney beans, edamame and chickpeas, nuts, dried apricots, fortified breakfast cereals, soy bean flour, dark‑green leafy vegetables such as watercress and kale, and pulses such as beans, peas and lentils (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron). The recommended daily intake is 8.7 mg for men aged 19 and over, 14.8 mg for women aged 19 to 49, and 8.7 mg for women aged 50 and over (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron). NHS notes that taking 17 mg or less of iron supplements per day is unlikely to be harmful (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron).

